course: dictionary
lesson: 8354
title: Rosin: a word from the violin case
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Rosin is a hard, sticky, amber-coloured substance. It is made from pine tree sap. Musicians, athletes and scientists all use the word, so the words around it give you clues to its meaning.
explain: «Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow so the hair grips the strings and makes them vibrate; traditional bow hair is horsehair.» Look at the clues: rubbed, bow, grips, strings. Something rubbed on a bow helps it grip, so rosin must be a sticky substance. Check by swapping in "sticky stuff" and reading the sentence again.
explain: Rosin can also be a verb. To rosin something means to rub rosin on it. If you read that a violinist rosined her bow, the -ed ending tells you it happened already, and the word bow tells you what was rosined.
explain: The place where you meet a word gives clues too. In music, rosin is the little block in the case, and without it the bow makes almost no sound. In sport, powdered rosin stops hands or shoes slipping. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Rosin is a hard, sticky, amber-coloured substance made from pine tree sap.
fact: Violin, viola, cello and double bass players rub rosin on the bow so the hairs grip the strings.
fact: Athletes such as gymnasts, dancers, baseball pitchers and climbers use powdered rosin to stop their hands or shoes slipping.
fact: To rosin something means to rub rosin on it.
fact: Useful phrases include "rosin the bow", "a block of rosin" and "rosin bag".
exercise: «Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow.» What is rosin? | A type of horsehair | *A hard, sticky, amber-coloured substance made from pine tree sap | A kind of violin string | A soft, white liquid from the sea
exercise: Which players rub rosin on the bow? | Dancers and gymnasts | *Violin, viola, cello and double bass players | Baseball pitchers only | Drummers and flute players
exercise: Athletes use powdered rosin for what purpose? | To make a loud sound | To make their hands slip | To warm up their muscles | *To stop their hands or shoes slipping
test: What does "to rosin something" mean? | *To rub rosin on it | To put it away in a case | To cut a string
test: «Without rosin, the bow makes almost no sound.» What does that tell you about rosin? | It makes the bow louder | *It helps the bow grip the strings so they make a sound | It turns the bow amber
exam: Which sentence uses rosin as a verb? | Horsehair is used for bows | Rosin is made from pine tree sap | *The violinist rosined her bow before the concert | The block of rosin sat in the case
